{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-141089" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-3{x}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-5x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141089" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-3{x}^{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{2})}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2x \\\\  & =-6x \\\\  & \\ne -5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=-3{x}^{2}$ and ${y}^{′}=-6x$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -6x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2(-3{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ -6x & =-6x\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=-3{x}^{2}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141089" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5"} {"question" "q-141089" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141089" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141089" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}}\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{x} \\\\  & \\ne \\frac{1}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{4}{x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡{x}^{2}}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{4}{x} & =\\frac{4}{x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y=4\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141088" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5"} {"question" "q-141088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141088" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x)}^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ and ${y}^{′}=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}y\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡2x \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & \\ne {cos}^{2}⁡2x\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141081" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5"} {"question" "q-141081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141081" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2y\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)={e}^{2x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x)={e}^{2x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={({e}^{2x})}^{′} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}^{′}=2{e}^{2x}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}2y \\\\ 2{e}^{2x} & =2{e}^{2x}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141078" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}},\\,x\\ne 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${f}^{′}(x)=-\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$\n- Substituting $y=f(x)$ and ${y}^{′}={f}^{′}(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Differentiating $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} \\\\  & \\ne -\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Substituting $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ and ${y}^{′}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}$ into the original equation, we get a false statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\ne -\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ is not a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141084" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5"} {"question" "q-141084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141084" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$\n- $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$\n- $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{x},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ 0 & \\ne \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{x}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ \\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{2(\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}} & =-\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=\\frac{4}{{x}^{2}}$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141099" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5"} {"question" "q-141099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141099" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141098" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{4y}{x},\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=4x$\n- $y=2{x}^{2}$\n- $y={x}^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141098" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=4x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ 4 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4(4x)}{x} \\\\ 4 & \\ne 16.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y=4x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2{x}^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=4x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ 4x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4(2{x}^{2})}{x} \\\\ 4x & \\overset{?}{=}8x \\\\ -4x & \\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=2{x}^{2}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{3},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{4y}{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4({x}^{3})}{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\ne 4{x}^{2} \\\\ 0 & \\ne {x}^{2}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{3}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141098" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5"} {"question" "q-141098" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141098" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141098" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3})}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-3})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-3}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & ={x}^{3} \\\\ -1 & ={x}^{4}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{3y}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{3(-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-6})}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{1}{{x}^{-6}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{x} & ={x}^{6} \\\\ -2 & ={x}^{7}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141103" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5"} {"question" "q-141103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141103" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "None"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}}.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{x}}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ 1 & =2\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{x} & =\\frac{2}{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true.* Therefore, the function $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{1}{x}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2}{{e}^{y/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{-1/2})}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2}{{x}^{-1/2}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{x} & =2\\sqrt{x} \\\\ -1 & =2x\\sqrt{x}.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y=-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141093" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5"} {"question" "q-141093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141093" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-79335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2y}{x}-2,\\,x\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y=2x$\n- $y={x}^{2}$\n- $y={x}^{3}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-79335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y=2x,$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot 2x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{4x}{x}-2 \\\\ 2 & \\overset{?}{=}4-2 \\\\ 2 & =2.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y=2x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{2},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{x}-2 \\\\ 2x & \\overset{?}{=}2x-2 \\\\ 0 & \\ne -2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y={x}^{2}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y={x}^{3},$ we get $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3{x}^{2}.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{2y}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2\\cdot {x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{x}-2 \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & \\overset{?}{=}2{x}^{2}-2 \\\\ {x}^{2} & \\ne -2\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *not true* for all $x.$ Therefore, the function $y={x}^{3}$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-79335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5"} {"question" "q-79335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-79335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-79335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-352655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4y,\\,y(1)=2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-352655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $y=A{e}^{-4x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(1)=2$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{e}^{-4x} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4\\cdot 1} \\\\ 2 & =A{e}^{-4} \\\\ 2{e}^{4} & =A \\\\ A & =2{e}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-352655" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5"} {"question" "q-352655" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-352655" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2e^{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-y},\\,y(1)=1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+x)$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(1)=1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+x) \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+1) \\\\ {e}^{1} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(A+1)} \\\\ e & =A+1 \\\\ A & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e-1+x).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141115" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5"} {"question" "q-141115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "2e-1"] ["math" "e+1"] ["math" "e-1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-141115" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "e-1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-347839" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=3y\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x),\\,y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.\n$$\n\nYou are given that $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-347839" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We are given that $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1$ into the solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ 1 & =Asin\\,(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\ 1 & =Asin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ 1 & =A(1) \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-347839" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5"} {"question" "q-347839" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-347839" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-352661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=3.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A-x}$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-352661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $y=\\frac{1}{A-x}$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(0)=3.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(0)=3$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{A-x} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{A-0} \\\\ 3 & =\\frac{1}{A} \\\\ A & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-352661" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5"} {"question" "q-352661" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-352661" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-352649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-2y\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x),\\,y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nYou are given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$\n\n$A=${{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-352649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We are given that $y=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{8})=\\sqrt{2}$ into the solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =Acos\\,(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\ \\sqrt{2} & =A\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-352649" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "560d0ead832ab0bfc59d6d138086f2ca1cd63c8fc973dec4ea5a09f6c4b83ea5"} {"question" "q-352649" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "q-352649" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/state.json" "file_sha256" "7552330881acdca125809115710e3a75656be7b416dc05d4c326a9ac897d7625"} {"question" "e-525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\ny' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0\n$$\n\n \n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n\n- Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/example-525.json" "file_sha256" "7e6262d3b9fbec801f9e62963f1f8bea696e40f1ff58144a3e657894a2706bfa"} {"question" "e-525" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Differentiating $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x},$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={(\\frac{1}{x})}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Substituting $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ and $y' = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$ into the original equation, we get a true statement:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & \\overset{?}{=}-\\frac{(\\frac{1}{x})}{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the function $y = \\dfrac{1}{x}$ is a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/example-525.json" "file_sha256" "7e6262d3b9fbec801f9e62963f1f8bea696e40f1ff58144a3e657894a2706bfa"} {"question" "e-9978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y.\n$$\n\n Which of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n- $y = e^x + 1$\n\n- $y = -e^x$\n\n- $y = 2e^x$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/example-9978.json" "file_sha256" "9709d8252b399a20a35aa99ab1334ca59e76ed265a4cdc799d841dd8195fa672"} {"question" "e-9978" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For a function $f(x)$ to be a solution to a differential equation, it must satisfy the equation for all values of $x$ in the equation's domain.\n\nWe check each proposed solution by differentiating and substituting it into the original equation. \n\n- Differentiating $y = e^x + 1,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ {e}^{x} & \\overset{?}{=}{e}^{x}+1 \\\\ 0 & \\ne 1.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false*. Therefore, the function $y = e^x+1$ is *not* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = -e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ -{e}^{x} & =-{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = -e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\n- Differentiating $y = 2e^x,$ we get $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = 2e^x.$ Substituting into the original equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =y \\\\ 2{e}^{x} & =2{e}^{x}.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis statement is *true*. Therefore, the function $y = 2e^x$ *is* a solution to our differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/example-9978.json" "file_sha256" "9709d8252b399a20a35aa99ab1334ca59e76ed265a4cdc799d841dd8195fa672"} {"question" "e-8732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3y}{x}, \\qquad y(-1) = -2.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/example-8732.json" "file_sha256" "a82952d638b5574698aa2f0b2aba862f5a7e3f3ec32068ef1443ed21a2bde1e5"} {"question" "e-8732" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =A{x}^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1)^{3} \\\\ -2 & =A(-1) \\\\ A & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = 2x^3 .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980992/example-8732.json" "file_sha256" "a82952d638b5574698aa2f0b2aba862f5a7e3f3ec32068ef1443ed21a2bde1e5"}] "usage" {"q-141078" [] "q-79335" [] "e-525" [] "e-9978" [] "e-8732" []} "basis" 878 "retractions" [[17592186101070 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n$y^{\\prime } = 2y$\n\nLet $f(x) = e^{2x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f^{\\prime }(x) = 2e^{2x}$\n2. Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y^{\\prime } = f^{\\prime }(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n3. The function $y = f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation"] [17592186101070 :question/worked-solution "The chain rule gives $f'(x)=2e^{2x}$, so I is true. The right-hand side is $2f(x)=2e^{2x}=f'(x)$ for all $x$, so II is true and $f$ is a solution, making III true. Select **I, II, and III**."] [17592186101451 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$\\frac{dy}{dx} = \\frac{2y}{x} - 2, x \\ne 0$.\n\nWhich of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n1. $y = 2x$\n2. $y = x^{2}$\n3. $y = x^{3}$"] [17592186101451 :question/worked-solution "For $x\\ne0$, compare each derivative with $2y/x-2$.\n\n- I: for $y=2x$, $y'=2$ and $2y/x-2=4-2=2$, so it works.\n- II: for $y=x^2$, $y'=2x$ but $2y/x-2=2x-2$, so it fails.\n- III: for $y=x^3$, $y'=3x^2$ but $2y/x-2=2x^2-2$, so it fails.\n\nSelect **I only**."] [17592186101451 :question/difficulty 17592186045463] [17592186100638 :question/problem "Consider the following differential equation.\n$y' = -\\dfrac{y}{x}, \\qquad x \\neq 0$\n\nLet $f(x) = \\dfrac{1}{x}$. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$\n2. Substituting $y = f(x)$ and $y' = f'(x)$ into the differential equation gives a true statement\n3. The function $y=f(x)$ is a solution of the differential equation"] [17592186100638 :question/worked-solution "For $x\\ne0$, differentiate $f(x)=x^{-1}$:\n$$f'(x)=-x^{-2}=-\\frac1{x^2}.$$\nThus statement I is true. Substitution into the right-hand side gives\n$$-\\frac{f(x)}x=-\\frac{1/x}x=-\\frac1{x^2}=f'(x),$$\nso II is true. This identity holds throughout either interval $x>0$ or $x<0$, making $f$ a solution and III true. The answer is **I, II, and III**."] [17592186100714 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = y$.\n\nWhich of the following is a solution to this equation?\n\n1. $y = e^x + 1$\n2. $y = -e^x$\n3. $y = 2e^x$"] [17592186100714 :question/worked-solution "A solution must satisfy $y'=y$ at every point of its interval. Differentiate each candidate and compare.\n\n- I: If $y=e^x+1$, then $y'=e^x\\ne e^x+1=y$. Therefore $y=e^x+1$ is not a solution.\n- II: If $y=-e^x$, then $y'=-e^x=y$. It is a solution.\n- III: If $y=2e^x$, then $y'=2e^x=y$. It is a solution.\n\nThe correct answer is **II and III only**."] [17592186100708 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{3y}{x}, \\qquad y(-1) = -2$.\n\nYou're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A$?"] [17592186100708 :question/worked-solution "We're given that $y = Ax^3$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(-1) = -2$.\n\nSubstituting the initial condition\n\n$$\ny({\\color{red}{-1}}) = {\\color{blue}{-2}}\n$$\n\ninto our solution and solving for $A$, we get\n\n$$\\begin{array}{rl}y & = A {x}^{3} \\\\ \\textcolor{blue}{- 2} & = A ( \\textcolor{red}{- 1} {)}^{3} \\\\ - 2 & = A ( - 1 ) \\\\ A & = 2.\\end{array}$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n$y = 2x^3$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186098191 17592186098192 17592186098193 17592186098194 17592186098195 17592186100303 17592186100304 17592186100305 17592186100306 17592186100307] "immutable_fields" [[17592186098190 :answer-field/correct] [17592186098190 :answer-field/id] [17592186098190 :answer-field/key] [17592186098190 :answer-field/type] [17592186100302 :answer-field/correct] [17592186100302 :answer-field/id] [17592186100302 :answer-field/key] [17592186100302 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
